ong queue and they're cross before I've said hello.' 2) 'Half the April lot still transfer anything they don't know.' 3) 'Since the price rise every call is an argument.' 4) 'I used to just sort out a wrong bill. Now half of them go off to BRT and the customer rings back.' 5) 'No breathing space between calls any more, occupancy is through the roof.' 6) 'Team leaders only care about handle time.'\n- Complaints log, monthly summary, Customer Relations (system-record): Complaints per 10,000 contacts: Jan 40, Feb 42, Mar 41, Apr 47, May 50, Jun 49, Jul 50. The complaint category field is optional on the logging form, and 64% of complaints since January are uncategorised. Of categorised complaints for April–July, the largest category is 'Price/tariff' (31%), followed by 'Service – delay' (24%) and 'Billing accuracy' (19%). Complaints are counted by the month received, not linked to the originating call.\n- Notes of conversation with Lewis Abara, team leader for the April-intake nesting group, 6 August (manager-account): 'My twelve are hitting handle time and their QA is improving week by week. I can't tell you their FCR. The dashboard only gives the site number, and MI produce agent-level FCR only on request. Honestly, on the floor they don't look much different to my old hands, but that's my impression, not data. I'd be gutted if they went on performance plans for a site-wide number.'\n\nBRIEF:\nFCR fell because of the 14 April Finance change, not the new starters. It cut the in-call credit limit from £100 to £25 and automatically refers larger billing adjustments to the back-office Billing Resolution team. Referred billing calls rose from 7% to 54% in the same week FCR dropped. Referred calls resolve at about 33% versus 82% for the rest, and 58% of repeat contacts after a referral asked about status. The April intake refers at the same rate as veteran agents, and veterans' FCR fell by the same amount (75% to 67%). The IVR redesign came two weeks after the drop and the